1
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:06,420
CONVERSATION 1
Mr. David Jackson, a staff writer at the New Yorker, is known for his non-fiction books of adventure.

2
00:01:06,420 --> 00:01:13,700
Today, we go on a different kind of adventure: Jackson's life of parenting his offspring.

3
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:23,580
David, as a parent of an 11-and a 14-year-old, what is the most interesting issue you are dealing with right now?

4
00:01:23,580 --> 00:01:29,960
It's easy to focus on the challenges, but so far,I find these ages to be kind of wonderful.

5
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:35,740
They are independent, and they have their own curiosities and obsessions.

6
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:40,780
You can talk to them about fairly sophisticated subject matter such as politics.

7
00:01:40,780 --> 00:01:47,060
Yes, that does sound refreshing compared with talking to younger children.

8
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:50,960
Do they ask you to proofread their essays?

9
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:52,960
Certainly, with writing they do.

10
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:56,020
I really just try to be encouraging.

11
00:01:56,020 --> 00:02:02,380
I think at this age, editorial guidance is less important than encouragement.

12
00:02:02,380 --> 00:02:08,980
Are there books that you think are important that your children read, and that all children read?

13
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:15,500
My general thought is to read widely and to incorporate a love for reading.

14
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:22,800
Learning to love to read, I think, is the optimal thing, because it gives you a skill you can take anywhere.

15
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:28,720
So you're not too concerned like some parents with the content they're reading?

16
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:31,800
I know I have some worries about that.

17
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:33,580
Yeah, read what you like.

18
00:02:33,580 --> 00:02:43,840
If a child loves graphic novels or comic books, whatever it is, that is turning them on to read and turning on their imagination.

19
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:49,280
I feel that children's tastes in books change as they reach adolescence.

20
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:52,880
I know that mine certainly did when I was a teenager.

21
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:54,520
What do you think?

22
00:02:54,520 --> 00:03:02,360
I think it's especially important as they get older to read subject matter that'll open their eyes to the world and people.

23
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:26,380
So I think both fiction and non-fiction are really important because they give you the power to begin to perceive the world through the lives of others.

24
00:03:26,380 --> 00:03:42,680
Q1. What do we learn about David Jackson from the conversation?

25
00:03:42,680 --> 00:04:00,940
Q2. What does the man think of young teenagers?

26
00:04:00,940 --> 00:04:20,160
Q3. How does the man help his kids with their essays?

27
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:40,080
Q4. What does the woman say about herself when she was a teenager?

28
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:50,880
CONVERSATION 2
In this episode of Money Talks, our guest is Molly Sanders,a university student and a successful young entrepreneur.

29
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:52,620
Molly, tell us about your business.

30
00:04:52,620 --> 00:05:05,900
Well, I sell specialty clothes through a website, mainly for women who have trouble finding suitable clothes in main street shops because of their height or weight.

31
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:09,000
But I do some men's clothes too.

32
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:13,340
How did you get started in this business at such a young age?

33
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:15,920
Are you studying fashion design?

34
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:20,100
Actually, I'm majoring in finance, but I've always loved clothes.

35
00:05:20,100 --> 00:05:24,380
And I started making my own at 14.

36
00:05:24,380 --> 00:05:28,220
Did you have any sort of training in design or sewing?

37
00:05:28,220 --> 00:05:31,400
Or was it a natural ability?

38
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:33,980
I'd have to say no to both.

39
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:40,640
No one taught me to make clothes and most of the things I made at first were disasters.

40
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:42,760
Why did you persevere?

41
00:05:42,760 --> 00:05:48,400
I think most people would give up if they kept failing, especially at that age.

42
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:50,760
I kept on out of necessity.

43
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:57,980
As you can see, I'm very tall and I couldn't find clothes that fit me in ordinary shops.

44
00:05:57,980 --> 00:06:02,520
So I kept trying and developed my skills over time.

45
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:11,980
Well, my notes say you earned $50,000 in profits last year, an extraordinary amount for a 20-year-old student.

46
00:06:11,980 --> 00:06:13,580
How did that happen?

47
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:17,420
Did you see a gap in the market and decide to fill it?

48
00:06:17,420 --> 00:06:22,920
No. When I started university, some classmates complimented my clothes.

49
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:30,360
And when I said I made them myself, other tall women started asking if I would make theirs.

50
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:31,580
And I did.

51
00:06:31,580 --> 00:06:34,760
And before I knew it, I was an entrepreneur.

52
00:06:34,760 --> 00:06:37,560
So what are your plans for the future?

53
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:39,940
Do you intend to open a physical store?

54
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:44,720
No. I'll keep things online to keep costs down.

55
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:52,240
But I will add more clothes for children, both girls and boys, and possibly even for infants.

56
00:06:52,240 --> 00:07:04,760
And I hope to add to my range of designs for men.

57
00:07:04,760 --> 00:07:21,900
Q5. What do we learn about the woman?

58
00:07:21,900 --> 00:07:39,800
Q6. What does the woman say about the clothes she made at first?

59
00:07:39,800 --> 00:08:00,020
Q7. Why did the woman persevere in making clothes for herself?

60
00:08:00,020 --> 00:08:49,920
Q8. What does the woman plan to do in the future?

61
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:58,160
PASSAGE 1
Researchers have identified a potent new antibiotic compound using artificial intelligence.

62
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:02,440
The antibiotic can kill very dangerous bacteria.

63
00:09:02,440 --> 00:09:15,240
According to a study published in the journal Cell, the compound successfully removed deadly strains of bacteria in mice which are resistant to all known antibiotics.

64
00:09:15,240 --> 00:09:24,980
The researchers say this is the first time that artificial intelligence has been used to find a powerful new antibiotic molecule.

65
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:26,740
Why does this matter?

66
00:09:26,740 --> 00:09:29,940
The answer is antibiotic resistance.

67
00:09:29,940 --> 00:09:37,260
This happens when bacteria develop the ability to survive the medications designed to kill them.

68
00:09:37,260 --> 00:09:43,680
Antibiotic resistance is a serious threat to health and the problem is growing.

69
00:09:43,680 --> 00:09:47,940
This makes finding new antibiotics very important.

70
00:09:47,940 --> 00:09:59,140
However, in recent decades, very few have been developed, and those that have tend to be very similar to drugs already available.

71
00:09:59,140 --> 00:10:10,080
The searches also tend to only focus on a narrow spectrum of chemical compounds, but this is where artificial intelligence comes in.

72
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:11,240
Why?

73
00:10:11,240 --> 00:10:18,360
To find new drugs, scientists screen molecules to predict how effective they might be.

74
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:24,960
Typically, such screening is done by humans in the lab, which is both costly and slow.

75
00:10:24,960 --> 00:10:28,000
Artificial intelligence is different.

76
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:32,380
It's fast, and it can process a high volume.

77
00:10:32,380 --> 00:10:42,520
It can screen hundreds of millions of compounds to identify a few interesting candidates that require experimental testing.

78
00:10:42,520 --> 00:10:49,500
Artificial intelligence is also able to predict if compounds are likely to be toxic.

79
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:56,640
Some experts assert that this work signifies a paradigm shift in antibiotic discovery.

80
00:10:56,640 --> 00:11:09,060
It could change drug discovery more generally.

81
00:11:09,060 --> 00:11:28,160
Q9. What have researchers done for the first time in history?

82
00:11:28,160 --> 00:11:46,720
Q10. What makes it important to find new antibiotic drugs?

83
00:11:46,720 --> 00:12:06,160
Q11. What does the passage say artificial intelligence is able to do in antibiotic research?

84
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:13,240
PASSAGE 2
A recent study overturned what we think we know about lying.

85
00:12:13,240 --> 00:12:18,580
Most of us have a theory about how to tell if someone is telling a lie.

86
00:12:18,580 --> 00:12:35,080
We may develop that theory from observations of those people we know well and see regularly, but we tend to generalize what we gather from that unscientific daily research and make it a universal theory.

87
00:12:35,080 --> 00:12:42,560
So we might imagine that liars have evasive eyes, or the opposite—they simply stare at you.

88
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:47,940
Or perhaps it is more generally nervous behavior we associate with lies.

89
00:12:47,940 --> 00:12:55,240
Whatever the particular theory, it's usually based on close observation of people we know.

90
00:12:55,240 --> 00:12:57,500
And we get lots of practice.

91
00:12:57,500 --> 00:13:02,400
On average, we're lied to some 200 times per day.

92
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:07,060
These are mostly harmless lies, but lies nonetheless.

93
00:13:07,060 --> 00:13:12,520
But there's a problem with our theories, even though they're based on all these observations.

94
00:13:12,520 --> 00:13:22,940
The average person you and me tested rigorously on how well we detect lies fails to do better than chance.

95
00:13:22,940 --> 00:13:32,460
That's well established over many studies and lots of attempts by researchers to work out reliable ways to detect lies.

96
00:13:32,460 --> 00:13:44,200
It's even relatively easy to fool lie detectors, the gold standard of lie detection, by training yourself in breathing techniques and symptom suppression.

97
00:13:44,200 --> 00:13:49,340
Is there any way to get better at detecting lies?

98
00:13:49,340 --> 00:13:53,080
The new research offers some surprising advice.

99
00:13:53,080 --> 00:13:55,880
Stop looking and listen instead.

100
00:13:55,880 --> 00:14:17,040
It turns out that if we're unable to see the face, but rather focus on the voice of the person in question, our accuracy rate improves considerably.

101
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:37,200
Q12. According to the passage, how do most people detect lying?

102
00:14:37,200 --> 00:14:55,820
Q13. What does the passage say about most lies?

103
00:14:55,820 --> 00:15:16,420
Q14. What have many studies uncovered about the average person's lie detection?

104
00:15:16,420 --> 00:16:08,550
Q15. What advice does the new research offer regarding lie detection?

105
00:16:08,550 --> 00:16:20,940
RECORDING 1
Appear to be submissive, humble, grateful and undemanding; show great pleasure when a doctor comes into your room, even if the visit is brief and useless.

106
00:16:20,940 --> 00:16:26,440
Don't challenge anyone with authority unless you are famous or very rich.

107
00:16:26,440 --> 00:16:33,040
Those are a few strategies for dealing with today's American medical establishment.

108
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:38,200
What patients want is to be treated with respect and consideration.

109
00:16:38,200 --> 00:16:44,380
But in my experience, too few hospitals and doctors are ready to do that.

110
00:16:44,380 --> 00:16:58,520
In his book, A Whole New Life, novelist Reynolds Price recalls that his doctors chose a crowded hallway as the place to tell him he might have a tumor on his spinal cord.

111
00:16:58,520 --> 00:17:08,100
It did not occur to the two physicians that a hallway was not the most appropriate place for that particular piece of news.

112
00:17:08,100 --> 00:17:12,760
My surgeon, who is in his mid-thirties, looks tired.

113
00:17:12,760 --> 00:17:18,160
He has been overwhelmed with patients who have fallen on the winter ice.

114
00:17:18,160 --> 00:17:23,340
He is a witty man, but sometimes his wit is unwelcome.

115
00:17:23,340 --> 00:17:34,440
"The health insurance company Blue Cross wants me to put you out in the snow tomorrow afternoon," he tells me after I have been in the hospital for more than a week.

116
00:17:34,440 --> 00:17:39,720
I'm terrified because I have no idea where to go.

117
00:17:39,720 --> 00:17:44,300
I cannot walk or even lift my leg a few inches.

118
00:17:44,300 --> 00:17:48,400
The hospital social worker strikes me as an idiot.

119
00:17:48,400 --> 00:17:52,960
But my complaints about her only annoy my surgeon.

120
00:17:52,960 --> 00:18:06,120
"I have to work with these people," he tells my friend, Dr. Karen Brudlney, when she mercifully intervenes on my behalf and arranges for me to be transferred to another hospital.

121
00:18:06,120 --> 00:18:11,460
"If you say one negative thing, they get defensive," she tells me later.

122
00:18:11,460 --> 00:18:15,300
"They have this kind of institutional loyalty.

123
00:18:15,300 --> 00:18:28,760
Always bring an advocate, that is, any other person with you to the hospital, and write down every single question and the answer, the name of every doctor and nurse.

124
00:18:28,760 --> 00:18:33,500
When people know you have their names, they behave better.

125
00:18:33,500 --> 00:18:48,020
And," Brudney adds, "if you, as a patient, suggest that you might like to control even part of the situation or be consulted or informed, then you are considered difficult.

126
00:18:48,020 --> 00:18:51,060
They want you to be totally passive.

127
00:18:51,060 --> 00:19:02,240
The entire health care system, particularly hospitals and nursing homes, exists for reasons that have nothing to do with taking care of patients.

128
00:19:02,240 --> 00:19:07,916
Patients are incidental."

129
00:19:07,916 --> 00:19:33,460
Q16. What does the speaker say about most American hospitals?

130
00:19:33,460 --> 00:19:51,820
Q17. What does Karen Brudney suggest patients do?

131
00:19:51,820 --> 00:20:11,920
Q18. What do American doctors expect their patients to be, according to Karen Brudney?

132
00:20:11,920 --> 00:20:19,660
RECORDING 2
There are probably teams you've worked with but you never want to work with again.

133
00:20:19,660 --> 00:20:26,520
But there must have also been other teams that you would prize reuniting with professionally.

134
00:20:26,520 --> 00:20:30,800
In other words, your team had vitality.

135
00:20:30,800 --> 00:20:41,360
Vitality comes about when the ties people form with their fellow team members are such that they stay connected even after the team breaks up.

136
00:20:41,360 --> 00:20:52,300
What characteristics of a team make its members more likely to stay in contact despite no longer working together?

137
00:20:52,300 --> 00:20:56,500
This question has been answered recently in a study publisbed in a business journal.

138
00:20:56,500 --> 00:21:02,300
One of the two key factors the research team discovered is sameness.

139
00:21:02,300 --> 00:21:06,860
Specifically, sharing the same gender or ethnic origin.

140
00:21:06,860 --> 00:21:17,740
The more members of a team share similar demographics, the more inclined they will be to remain associates long after the team has served its purpose.

141
00:21:17,740 --> 00:21:22,880
After ties are established, similarity strengthens them.

142
00:21:22,880 --> 00:21:33,980
As a result, they regard these individuals with greater trust and mutual understanding, which motivates them to seek further opportunities for collaboration.

143
00:21:33,980 --> 00:21:41,700
In effect, people tend to create stronger and longer-lasting connections with similar others.

144
00:21:41,700 --> 00:21:48,480
Someone who looks and sounds different from us may have the resources we need to be more successful.

145
00:21:48,480 --> 00:21:54,960
Yet, we find then to be significantly less credible simply because they are different.

146
00:21:54,960 --> 00:22:03,140
If you are a fierce advocate of workplace diversity, you'll no doubt be horrified by such a revelation.

147
00:22:03,140 --> 00:22:10,720
The second factor identified by the researchers is the quality of the relationships among the team members.

148
00:22:10,720 --> 00:22:25,400
The more they trust one another, share the same goals and depend on each other for the achievement of those goals, the stronger their chances of maintaining their connections, despite no longer working as one team.

149
00:22:25,400 --> 00:22:37,820
Teams with quality relationships have a shared belief that it's safe to take risks with each ether, and their members are obliged to share the workload and help out.

150
00:22:37,820 --> 00:22:43,900
From personal experience, I can see both the truth and the inconsistency of such studies.

151
00:22:43,900 --> 00:22:56,440
The truth is some of my closest friendships were formed as a result of having worked together on teams, and I actively seek opportunities to work with them again.

152
00:22:56,440 --> 00:23:05,920
The inconsistency, though, is that I've never worked for a team more successful and cohesive than the one of which I am a member right now.

153
00:23:05,920 --> 00:23:11,660
And yet, the four of us have very little in common and are completely different demographically.

154
00:23:11,660 --> 00:23:24,860
So I am unlikely to question the value of a diverse workforce.

155
00:23:24,860 --> 00:23:45,760
Q19. What does the speaker say about a team with vitality?

156
00:23:45,760 --> 00:24:05,280
Q20. What do the researchers find out about members of a team?

157
00:24:05,280 --> 00:24:24,520
Q21. What do we learn about the team the speaker is currently working in?

158
00:24:24,520 --> 00:24:34,400
RECORDING 3
An American researcher who studied 600 millionaires found how rich you can get comes down to six wealth factors.

159
00:24:34,400 --> 00:24:43,140
She found that six behaviors are related to net worth potential, regardless of age or income.

160
00:24:43,140 --> 00:24:51,860
These were thriftiness, confidence, responsibility, planning, focus and social indifference.

161
00:24:51,860 --> 00:24:55,400
Being thrifty comes as no great surprise.

162
00:24:55,400 --> 00:25:06,270
Spending above your means, spending instead of saving for retirement,spending in anticipation of becoming wealthy,makes you a slave to the paycheck.

163
00:25:06,270 --> 00:25:19,350
"Even with an astronomical level of income," she wrote, "to properly build wealth, experts recommend saving 20% of your income and living off the remaining 80%."

164
00:25:19,350 --> 00:25:25,030
Having confidence is another key characteristic, as it helps people to be thrifty.

165
00:25:25,030 --> 00:25:29,110
It takes confidence to live within your means.

166
00:25:29,110 --> 00:25:32,590
It also takes confidence to invest properly.

167
00:25:32,590 --> 00:25:44,910
Instead of making investing decisions with your emotions, financial planners advise that you should leave your investrnents alone and focus on a long term investment plan.

168
00:25:44,910 --> 00:25:52,950
But people can't invest or manage their own money without accepting responsibility for the outcomes.

169
00:25:52,950 --> 00:26:04,830
Many millionaires take on personal responsibility, and most also happen to be self-made, meaning they didn't acquire their wealth through luck.

170
00:26:04,830 --> 00:26:13,010
Millionaires don't count on anyone else to make them rich, and they don't blame anyone else if they fall short.

171
00:26:13,010 --> 00:26:21,200
They focus on things they can control and align their daily habits to the goals they have set for themselves.

172
00:26:21,200 --> 00:26:31,640
They tend to be goal-oriented and hard workers, which enables them to plan financially and focus on seeing those plans through.

173
00:26:31,640 --> 00:26:44,260
92% of the milionaires surveyed developed a longterm plan for their money and 97% almost always achieved the goals they set for themselves.

174
00:26:44,260 --> 00:26:50,020
And it is these behaviors that make it easy for them to be socially indifferent.

175
00:26:50,020 --> 00:26:56,800
They resist lifestyle creep, the tendency to spend more whenever one earns more.

176
00:26:56,800 --> 00:27:04,820
Essentially, they don't yield to pressure to buy the latest thing or to keep up with others or what they have acquired.

177
00:27:04,820 --> 00:27:25,842
Instead of being focused on what might make them happy today, they're focused on their long-term wealth-building plan.

178
00:27:25,842 --> 00:27:42,660
Q22. What do experts recommend concerning being thrifty?

179
00:27:42,660 --> 00:28:01,400
Q23. How does confidence help people to be thrifty?

180
00:28:01,400 --> 00:28:21,180
Q24. How do millionaires react when they fail in their investment?

181
00:28:21,180 --> 00:28:42,364
Q25. Why does the speaker say millionaires are socially indifferent?
